---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";

const title = "Best Treadmills Under $300 (2026): What This Budget Really Buys";
const description =
  "Five walking pads and 2-in-1 machines we saw selling for $150 to $300, checked against brand listings and independent coverage — and a plain account of what this budget cannot buy.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "urevo-spacewalk-3s",
    name: "UREVO SpaceWalk 3S",
    category: "Best Overall Under $300",
    badge: "Best Overall",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=UREVO+SpaceWalk+3S+Walking+Pad&tag=sktiger-20",
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$250–$270",
    specs: "0–9% powered incline · 0.6–4.0 mph · 42.5 in. belt length · 265 lb capacity · 6.5 in. tall",
    pros: [
      "Powered incline is rare at this price, and it is the only way to make a 4 mph pad harder",
      "The longest belt on this page at 42.5 inches, comfortable for taller walkers",
      "Brand page and independent coverage agree on speed, belt, incline and capacity",
      "Selling at $249.99 on UREVO’s store against a $349.99 list price when we checked",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Walking only: 4 mph is the ceiling",
      "Advertised 2.5 HP is a peak figure; independent coverage derives well under 1 HP continuous from the manual",
      "Warranty is one year, with the extra 180 days depending on app registration",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The best way to spend $250 on indoor walking: a long belt, a powered incline and figures that check out.",
    description: "The SpaceWalk 3S is the pad that gets the most out of a $300 budget, because it adds the one feature that keeps walking interesting once you reach a pad’s 4 mph ceiling: a powered incline. UREVO lists 0 to 9% across nine levels, changed from the remote or the app while you walk, on a 42.5 by 16.5-inch belt, with a 0.6 to 4.0 mph speed range, a 265 lb capacity and a height of 6.5 inches. An independent tracker that reads the manuals agrees on each of those. The longer belt is the quiet advantage: on a short pad, raising the front shortens your stride, and on this one it does not. The motor figure is the usual story — 2.5 HP advertised, a fraction of that continuous — and it is a walking machine only. UREVO’s store had it at $249.99, the tracker had last seen it at about $270, and it has rarely sold near its list price. For daily desk walking with some variety, it is the pick.",
  },
  {
    id: "urevo-strol-2e",
    name: "UREVO Strol 2E",
    category: "Best Under $300 for Light Jogging",
    badge: "Best 2-in-1",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=UREVO+Strol+2E+Folding+Treadmill&tag=sktiger-20",
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$170–$250",
    specs: "0.6–6.2 mph with bar raised · 40.2 x 15 in. belt · 265 lb capacity · fold-down handlebar · no incline",
    pros: [
      "Raise the front bar and the top speed goes from 4 mph to 6.2 mph, enough for a gentle jog",
      "A bar to hold, which steadies a walk at speed and most pads lack",
      "Folds flat to under 5 inches for storage under a bed or sofa",
      "Often under $200 — UREVO’s own store had it at $169.99 when we checked",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Flat deck only; an Amazon listing under the same name that claims 12% incline is describing the Strol 2E Pro",
      "A 40-inch, 15-inch-wide belt is short and narrow for a jogging stride",
      "The 2.25 HP figure is advertised; the derived continuous rating is under 1 HP, so jogging should stay occasional",
    ],
    bottomLine: "For walkers who want the option of a few minutes’ light jogging and a bar to hold, on the smallest budget that buys both.",
    description: "The Strol 2E is the 2-in-1 to buy if you want more than a walk now and then. Folded flat, it is a 0.6 to 4 mph desk pad; with the front bar raised, its top speed rises to 6.2 mph, which is a light jog for most adults. UREVO’s page, an independent walking pad tracker and published reviews agree on that range, on a 40.2 by 15-inch belt and on a 265 lb capacity. It comes ready to use with a remote, dual LED displays and app support, and folds to under 5 inches tall. Be realistic about the jogging mode. The belt is short and narrow for a running stride, the motor’s continuous rating is far below the 2.25 HP on the box, and reviewers flag the belt width for taller users. It is a walking pad that can jog briefly, not a treadmill. Check the listing carefully: one Amazon listing using this name describes a 12% incline and 300 lb rating that belong to the Strol 2E Pro.",
  },
  {
    id: "urevo-spacewalk-e1l",
    name: "UREVO SpaceWalk E1L",
    category: "Cheapest Walking Pad We Could Verify",
    badge: "Lowest Price",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=UREVO+SpaceWalk+E1L+Walking+Pad&tag=sktiger-20",
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$145–$170",
    specs: "0.6–4.0 mph · 35.4 in. belt length · no incline · no handrail",
    pros: [
      "The lowest price of any pad on this page from a brand with its own store and warranty process",
      "About 4 inches tall and under 37 lb, so it slides under almost anything",
      "Arrives assembled with a remote and app support",
      "Simple: no incline motor or folding bar, so there is less to go wrong",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Capacity is listed as 265 lb by UREVO but 242 lb in the documentation an independent tracker read, so we have printed neither",
      "A 35-inch belt is short; taller walkers may find their stride cramped",
      "Flat and capped at 4 mph, so there is no way to make it harder as you get fitter",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The pad to buy if you want the cheapest sensible way to walk at a desk and you are of average height or shorter.",
    description: "If your budget is closer to $150 than $300, the E1L is the pad we would buy. UREVO lists a 0.6 to 4.0 mph speed range and a 35.4 by 15-inch belt, it stands about 4 inches tall and weighs under 37 lb, and independent coverage agrees on the speed and belt. It arrives assembled, runs from a remote or the UREVO app, and has nothing to adjust. UREVO’s own store had it at $169.99 when we checked, Amazon was selling it for about $145 as part of a bundle listing, and one major retailer has had it under $130 on sale. The compromise is the belt: 35 inches suits walkers of average height at desk pace, but a long stride will feel cramped. The figure we could not settle is capacity. UREVO says 265 lb; an independent tracker reading the documentation found 242 lb. Assume the lower one, and if you are anywhere near it, spend more. The warranty is one year plus a 180-day extension with app registration.",
  },
  {
    id: "deerrun-z10",
    name: "DeerRun Z10",
    category: "Best Budget Powered Incline",
    badge: "Budget Incline",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=DeerRun+Z10+Walking+Pad+Incline&tag=sktiger-20",
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$200–$240",
    specs: "12-level powered incline · 0.6–3.8 mph · 35 x 16 in. belt · 300 lb capacity",
    pros: [
      "A motorised incline for $200–$240, where most pads are flat",
      "300 lb capacity, the highest on this page, stated consistently by the brand, the listing and independent coverage",
      "A hands-on reviewer found the frame stable at full incline",
      "Under 6 inches tall level, so it suits most standing desks",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Sold with 12 incline levels, but a hands-on reviewer measured about 9% at the top, so do not count on 12%",
      "A 35-inch belt is short, and incline makes it feel shorter",
      "One-year warranty, and an independent tracker reports a C- rating with the Better Business Bureau and polarised owner reviews",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The cheapest way to add a motorised incline to a desk pad. Best for shorter walkers who will check the warranty and returns terms first.",
    description: "The Z10 earns its place by bringing a powered incline down to about $200–$240. The brand listing, the Amazon listing and independent coverage agree on 12 levels of motorised incline controlled from a remote or app, a 0.6 to 3.8 mph speed range, a 35 by 16-inch belt and a 300 lb capacity, the highest consistent rating on this page. Where they part company is the top of the incline: the listing implies 12%, and one reviewer who used the pad measured about 9% at the highest level. That is still a useful walking grade, but it is not the number on the box, so we have left the percentage out. The same reviewer found the frame stable and flagged the short belt as the main drawback. The brushless motor and 3 HP figure are marketing claims we could not confirm. DeerRun is a marketplace brand with a one-year warranty and mixed feedback on support, so register the pad and keep the receipt.",
  },
  {
    id: "wellfit-tm037",
    name: "Wellfit TM037",
    category: "Best Under $300 With a Handrail",
    badge: "With Handrail",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Wellfit+TM037+Walking+Pad&tag=sktiger-20",
    reviewUrl: "/wellfit-tm037-treadmill/",
    priceRange: "$140–$170",
    specs: "36 x 16 in. belt · 0.6–6.2 mph · 0–5% manual incline · fold-up handrail",
    pros: [
      "A full fold-up handrail rather than a single bar, the most support of any machine here",
      "Manual incline to about 5%, set before you start",
      "Lists the same 6.2 mph top speed as the Strol 2E, for brief jogging with the rail up",
      "The lowest sale prices on this page, at around $140–$170 when we checked",
    ],
    cons: [
      "The advertised brushless 3.0 HP motor is contradicted by an independent teardown report describing a brushed motor rated far lower",
      "Warranty terms differ between Wellfit’s page and the Amazon listing; the motor is effectively covered for one year",
      "An independent tracker reports an F rating with the Better Business Bureau and complaints about refused returns",
    ],
    bottomLine: "For light daily walking with a rail to hold and a little incline, at the lowest price here. Get the warranty and returns terms in writing before you order.",
    description: "The TM037 is the only machine on this page with a proper handrail, and that is its case. The rail folds down for desk use and up for walking with support, and the deck props to as much as 5% incline, set by hand before a session. Wellfit’s page and the Amazon listing agree on a 36 by 16-inch belt, a 0.6 to 6.2 mph speed range and the 0 to 5% incline, and an independent tracker matches them. It was selling for about $140–$170 against a $399.99 list price when we checked. The cautions are serious enough to state plainly. An independent teardown reported a brushed motor with a far lower continuous rating than the advertised brushless 3.0 HP; the warranty wording differs between Wellfit’s page and Amazon; and the same tracker reports an F rating with the Better Business Bureau and a pattern of refused returns. Our full review of the TM037 goes through each. Buy it through a channel whose own return terms protect you, and treat it as a light-use machine.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "By Price", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/price/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Best Treadmills Under $300", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/best-treadmills-under-300/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can you get a good treadmill for under $300?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "You can get a good walking pad for under $300, and for indoor walking that is often the better machine. What you cannot get is a running treadmill: the belts at this price are 35 to 42 inches long, the motors are rated well below their headline figures, and the warranties are about a year. If you want to run regularly, save for a machine with a 55-inch belt.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a walking pad or a cheap folding treadmill better under $300?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "At this price, almost always the walking pad. A pad spends the money on the belt and motor; a folding treadmill at the same price spends much of it on an upright and frame. The cheapest folding treadmills with side rails we could verify were above $300 anyway. Choose a 2-in-1 pad with a fold-up bar if you want something to hold.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can you run on a treadmill under $300?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Only briefly and lightly. Two machines here reach 6.2 mph with the bar raised, which is a gentle jog, but their belts are about 36 to 40 inches long and their motors are rated far below the box figure. Treat jogging as a few minutes at the end of a walk. A regular running habit needs a full treadmill with a 55 to 60-inch deck.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How long does a $300 treadmill last?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No one publishes reliable lifespan data for pads, so be wary of anyone who quotes a number. The manufacturers on this page warranty their motors for about a year, which tells you what they are prepared to stand behind. Light use, staying well under the weight limit and lubricating the belt on schedule make the biggest difference to how long one lasts.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why do budget treadmill specs differ between listings?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Budget pads are sold under the same name on a brand store, Amazon and other marketplaces, with listings written separately and figures revised without a new model name. Titles also borrow specifications from sibling models. Motor horsepower, weight capacity and incline disagree most. Check the brand's own product page and confirm any figure that matters with the seller in writing.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What weight capacity do I need in a cheap treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Choose a machine rated at least 50 lb above your weight, and treat the printed capacity as a ceiling rather than an operating point. At this price, capacities often differ between the listing and the manual. The highest figure that held up in our checks was 300 lb, so anyone over about 250 lb should look at full treadmills with consistent, published ratings.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Morgan Reyes" },
      datePublished: "2026-10-13",
      dateModified: "2026-10-13",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: { "@type": "Brand", name: product.name.split(" ")[0] },
      offers: {
        "@type": "AggregateOffer",
        priceCurrency: "USD",
        availability: "https://schema.org/InStock",
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-10-13",
    modifiedTime: "2026-10-13",
    author: "Morgan Reyes",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "best treadmill under 300",
      "treadmill under 300",
      "cheap treadmill",
      "budget treadmill",
      "walking pad under 300",
      "urevo strol 2e",
      "urevo spacewalk 3s",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "See Our Top Picks", link: "#picks" }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <svg class="absolute inset-0 w-full h-full opacity-10" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
        <pattern id="dot-pattern-besttreadmillsunder300" x="0" y="0" width="24" height="24" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse">
          <circle cx="2" cy="2" r="1.5" fill="white"></circle>
        </pattern>
        <rect width="100%" height="100%" fill="url(#dot-pattern-besttreadmillsunder300)"></rect>
      </svg>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center gap-3 mb-6 flex-wrap">
          <span class="bg-[#FF5A1F] text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Buying Guide
          </span>
          <span class="bg-green-500 text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Updated October 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Best Treadmills <span class="text-[#5AA9FF]">Under $300</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
          Under $300 you are buying a walking machine. Some of them fold up a bar and let you jog for a few minutes; none of them is a running treadmill, and none will last like one. Within those limits, here are five worth the money, and the listing claims to ignore while you shop.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-4">
          <a href="#picks" class="bg-[#FF5A1F] hover:bg-[#E64F16] text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm">
            See the Picks
          </a>
          <a href="#what-300-buys" class="bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm border border-white/20">
            What $300 Cannot Buy
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>


    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4 py-8 border-b border-gray-100">
        <img src="https://i.pravatar.cc/150?img=48" alt="Morgan Reyes" class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full" />
        <div>
          <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 leading-tight">Morgan Reyes</p>
          <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm leading-tight">Fitness Equipment Editor</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block w-px h-8 bg-gray-200"></div>
        <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm">
          Last Updated: <strong class="text-gray-700">October 13, 2026</strong>
        </p>
        <div class="ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="3.1k" />
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="not-prose my-10 bg-blue-50 border border-blue-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 m-0 leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-gray-900">On the products below.</strong> We have not tested these
          machines. Each was confirmed on 13 October 2026 as currently sold, and its specifications
          were checked on that date against the brand's own product page (UREVO, DeerRun and
          Wellfit), the Amazon listing, and independent coverage — chiefly a long-running walking
          pad tracker that reads owner's manuals and motor labels, plus published reviews. A figure
          appears in a product's specification line only where those sources agree. Budget
          listings at this price disagree with each other often, and where they did — on motor
          horsepower, weight capacity, maximum incline, unit weight and warranty — we left the
          figure out and say so in the text. None of these are our own measurements. Prices are
          those we saw on brand stores, Amazon and price trackers on 13 October 2026; at this end
          of the market they move weekly and "list prices" are often inflated, so confirm the
          price, the specification and the warranty terms against the exact unit before you order.
          We earn a commission if you buy through the Amazon links on this page; it does not change
          which products we list or what we say about them.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-2 text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mt-0 mb-4">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-5 h-5 text-[#FF5A1F]" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h7"></path></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#quick-picks" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">1. Our Picks at a Glance</a>
          <a href="#picks" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">2. The 5 Best Treadmills Under $300</a>
          <a href="#how-we-chose" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">3. How We Chose These Machines</a>
          <a href="#what-300-buys" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">4. What $300 Buys, and What It Cannot</a>
          <a href="#running" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">5. Can You Run on a $300 Machine?</a>
          <a href="#longevity" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">6. How Long a $300 Machine Lasts</a>
          <a href="#reading-listings" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">7. Reading a Budget Listing: The Numbers to Distrust</a>
          <a href="#who-should-skip" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">8. Who Should Spend Differently</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">9. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Search for a treadmill under $300 and you will find hundreds of listings promising
          running speeds, 3 HP motors and 300 lb ratings. Almost all of them are walking pads, many
          from brands with no presence outside a marketplace, and their specifications frequently
          contradict their own manuals. This page does two things: it names five machines in this
          price band whose basic figures held up when we checked them, and it says plainly what a
          $300 budget can and cannot buy, so you can decide whether to spend it at all.
        </p>

        <div id="quick-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Our Picks at a Glance</h2>
<p>
            Five machines, all of them walking pads or 2-in-1 pads with a fold-up bar, because that
            is what this budget buys. The short version: the UREVO SpaceWalk 3S for most people,
            the UREVO Strol 2E if you want to jog occasionally, and the UREVO SpaceWalk E1L if you
            want the cheapest pad we could verify.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Pick</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Best for</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Why</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">UREVO SpaceWalk 3S</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Most people</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Powered 0–9% incline on a 42.5-inch belt for about $250</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">UREVO Strol 2E</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">An occasional light jog</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Raise the bar and it reaches 6.2 mph; about $170–$250</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">UREVO SpaceWalk E1L</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">The lowest price</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">A plain, slim desk pad for around $145–$170</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">DeerRun Z10</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Powered incline on a budget</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">12-level motorised incline and a 300 lb rating for $200–$240</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Wellfit TM037</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Something to hold</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Full fold-up handrail and manual incline for $140–$170</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            If you were hoping for a folding treadmill with side rails and a running deck, read
            <a href="#what-300-buys" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">what $300 cannot buy</a>
            before anything else. It will save you from the most common disappointment at this
            price.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. The 5 Best Treadmills Under $300</h2>
<p>
            Two flat pads, two with incline and one 2-in-1 with a proper handrail, each with a
            clear reason to choose it. The specification line on each card carries only figures that
            the brand's own listing and at least one independent source agree on. Motor horsepower
            is missing from all five for the same reason: on every one, the advertised figure did
            not match the manual, the motor label or both.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

